(ns kotobase-peer.ldbc-snb-bench
  "LDBC SNB Interactive complex reads IC02 / IC07 / IC08 / IC09 against
  kotobase's hot db and against Neo4j embedded, in the SAME JVM, over the
  SAME real LDBC dataset.

  Why: every prior kotobase head-to-head receipt (2026-07-25 Datomic /
  Neo4j / RisingWave) measured a SINGLE-ATTRIBUTE point lookup and a full
  scan, and said so -- \"no joins, no history queries, no concurrent load --
  this is a first receipt, not a TPC substitute\". Those are the cheapest
  queries a graph engine can be asked. NamiDB (namidb.com/benchmarks)
  publishes exactly these four LDBC SNB Interactive queries against Kuzu and
  Neo4j, so measuring the same four here is what makes the two projects'
  numbers comparable at all -- and Neo4j, measured on BOTH sides, is the
  common yardstick that lets a ratio survive the different machines.

  Data: the real LDBC SNB Interactive v1 SF1 dataset (Datagen v1.0.0,
  CsvComposite/LongDateFormatter, from the SURF/CWI repository), NOT a
  synthetic imitation. SF1 in full is ~10.5M elements, far more than one
  session can fold, so a DETERMINISTIC, STATED subset is loaded:

    * ALL 9,892 persons and ALL 180,623 `knows` edges -- the social topology
      that IC02/IC09's fan-out depends on is preserved exactly, not sampled.
    * the `posts` most recent Posts by creationDate, plus EVERY Comment that
      transitively replies into that set (whole conversation trees, so IC08's
      reply lookup and IC07's likes have real depth, not truncated stubs).
    * `hasCreator` for every included Message, and every `likes` edge whose
      target Message is included.

  Two modelling asymmetries, stated rather than averaged away:
    * `knows` is stored ONE direction in the CSV and is undirected in the
      LDBC spec. Neo4j matches it undirected natively (`-[:KNOWS]-`);
      kotobase's datom model has no undirected edge, so BOTH directions are
      asserted (2x the quads). This is what a real kotobase app would do.
    * LDBC's `likes` edge carries a creationDate PROPERTY. Neo4j puts it on
      the relationship; kotobase has no edge properties, so each like is
      REIFIED into an entity with 3 datoms. Both the element counts and the
      IC07 cost reflect that -- it is a genuine data-model cost, not overhead
      the harness invented.
    * kotobase's query language has no ORDER BY / LIMIT (arrangement.datalog
      is a conjunctive Datalog with aggregates, no ordering), so the
      top-20-by-date step runs in harness Clojure over the join's full
      result. Neo4j does it inside Cypher. Both sides are timed to the SAME
      final answer, and the answers are asserted EQUAL before any timing is
      reported -- but the work is not distributed the same way, and that is
      itself a finding about kotobase's query surface.

  Usage: clojure -M:ldbc-snb-bench <ldbc-dynamic-dir> [posts] [samples] [params-dir]
  With `params-dir` (substitution_parameters-sf<N> plus short_message_param.txt)
  every served read is also compared with Neo4j over every official parameter
  -- the reference the network rows cite."
  (:require [kotobase-peer.core :as eng]
            [arrangement.core :as qs]
            [kotoba.lang.text :as str]
            [clojure.java.io :as io])
  (:import [org.neo4j.dbms.api DatabaseManagementServiceBuilder]
           [org.neo4j.graphdb Label]
           [java.nio.file Files]
           [java.nio.file.attribute FileAttribute]
           [java.util.concurrent TimeUnit]))

;; ── CSV ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- reduce-rows
  "Stream one LDBC composite CSV through `rf` (header dropped), splitting each
  line only as it is consumed. The comment and likes files are 2M+ rows each;
  realising them as a seq of vectors was costing multiple GB of heap for data
  the subset immediately discards."
  [dir file rf init]
  (let [f (io/file dir file)]
    (when-not (.exists f)
      (throw (ex-info "LDBC csv missing" {:file (.getPath f)})))
    (with-open [r (io/reader f)]
      (reduce (fn [acc line] (rf acc (str/split line #"\|" -1)))
              init (rest (line-seq r))))))

(defn- rows
  "Fully realised rows -- only for the small files (person, knows)."
  [dir file]
  (reduce-rows dir file conj []))

(defn- load-subset
  "Real LDBC SF1, subset per the ns docstring. Two passes on purpose: the
  reply forest is resolved from the (small-per-row) replyOf files first, so
  the 2M-row comment body file is streamed once and only the kept rows are
  retained."
  [dir n-posts]
  (let [persons (into {} (map (fn [[id fname lname & _]] [id [fname lname]]))
                      (rows dir "person_0_0.csv"))
        ;; profile fields (IS1) and friendship dates (IS3), ayatori iteration 24
        profiles (into {} (map (fn [[id _ _ gender birthday cd ip browser & _]]
                                 [id {:gender gender :birthday (parse-long birthday) :creationDate (parse-long cd)
                                      :locationIP ip :browserUsed browser}]))
                       (rows dir "person_0_0.csv"))
        city (into {} (map (fn [[p place]] [p place])) (rows dir "person_isLocatedIn_place_0_0.csv"))
        knows (mapv (fn [[a b cd]] [a b (parse-long cd)]) (rows dir "person_knows_person_0_0.csv"))
        ;; posts: keep the n-posts most recent by creationDate
        kept-posts (->> (reduce-rows dir "post_0_0.csv"
                                     (fn [acc [id _img cd _ip _br _lang content _len]]
                                       (conj! acc [id (parse-long cd) content]))
                                     (transient []))
                        persistent! (sort-by second >) (take n-posts) vec)
        post-ids (into #{} (map first) kept-posts)
        reply-post (reduce-rows dir "comment_replyOf_post_0_0.csv"
                                (fn [acc [c p]] (if (post-ids p) (conj! acc c) acc))
                                (transient []))
        reply-comment (persistent!
                       (reduce-rows dir "comment_replyOf_comment_0_0.csv"
                                    (fn [acc [c p]] (conj! acc [c p])) (transient [])))
        ;; fixpoint over the reply forest: a comment is kept if its parent is kept
        kept-comments
        (loop [frontier (set (persistent! reply-post)) kept #{}]
          (if (empty? frontier)
            kept
            (let [kept' (into kept frontier)
                  nxt (into #{} (comp (filter (fn [[_ parent]] (frontier parent)))
                                      (map first)
                                      (remove kept'))
                            reply-comment)]
              (recur nxt kept'))))
        comment-meta (persistent!
                      (reduce-rows dir "comment_0_0.csv"
                                   (fn [acc [id cd _ip _br content _len]]
                                     (if (kept-comments id)
                                       (assoc! acc id [(parse-long cd) content])
                                       acc))
                                   (transient {})))
        keep-pairs (fn [file pred]
                     (persistent!
                      (reduce-rows dir file
                                   (fn [acc [a b]] (if (pred a) (assoc! acc a b) acc))
                                   (transient {}))))
        keep-likes (fn [file pred]
                     (persistent!
                      (reduce-rows dir file
                                   (fn [acc [p m cd]]
                                     (if (pred m) (conj! acc [p m (parse-long cd)]) acc))
                                   (transient []))))]
    {:persons persons
     :knows knows
     :messages (into (into {} (map (fn [[id cd content]] [id [cd content]])) kept-posts)
                     (map (fn [c] [c (comment-meta c)])) kept-comments)
     :post-ids post-ids
     :comment-ids kept-comments
     :creator (merge (keep-pairs "post_hasCreator_person_0_0.csv" post-ids)
                     (keep-pairs "comment_hasCreator_person_0_0.csv" kept-comments))
     :reply-of (into (into {} (filter (fn [[c p]] (and (kept-comments c) (post-ids p))))
                           (persistent!
                            (reduce-rows dir "comment_replyOf_post_0_0.csv"
                                         (fn [acc [c p]] (conj! acc [c p])) (transient []))))
                     (filter (fn [[c p]] (and (kept-comments c) (kept-comments p))))
                     reply-comment)
     :likes (into (keep-likes "person_likes_post_0_0.csv" post-ids)
                  (keep-likes "person_likes_comment_0_0.csv" kept-comments))
     :profiles profiles
     :city city
     ;; forums (IS6): the forum of every kept Post, its title, creationDate
     ;; and moderator -- only forums that contain a kept post are loaded
     :forums (let [post->forum (persistent!
                                (reduce-rows dir "forum_containerOf_post_0_0.csv"
                                             (fn [acc [f post]] (if (post-ids post) (assoc! acc post f) acc))
                                             (transient {})))
                   wanted (set (vals post->forum))
                   moderator (persistent!
                              (reduce-rows dir "forum_hasModerator_person_0_0.csv"
                                           (fn [acc [f p]] (if (wanted f) (assoc! acc f p) acc))
                                           (transient {})))
                   meta (persistent!
                         (reduce-rows dir "forum_0_0.csv"
                                      (fn [acc [f title cd]] (if (wanted f) (assoc! acc f [title (parse-long cd)]) acc))
                                      (transient {})))]
               {:post->forum post->forum
                :forums (into {} (map (fn [[f [title cd]]] [f {:title title :creationDate cd :moderator (get moderator f)}])) meta)})}))

;; ── kotobase side ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- build-kotobase
  "Hot db built via `eng/transact` -- the same db VALUE the persisted path
  produces (`hydrate-db` reduces `assert-quad` over cold rows into
  `qs/empty-db`; `transact` is that same assert), without paying this
  subset's AES-GCM + content-addressing write cost, which is measured
  separately in the merkle-lsm receipts and is not what an IC query's
  latency depends on. Stated, not hidden: no crypto and no persistence in
  THIS number."
  [{:keys [persons knows messages creator reply-of likes profiles city forums]}]
  (let [q (fn [s p o] {:s s :p p :o o})
        ;; a LAZY seq of quads, transacted in batches: one transact over 20M
        ;; quads (full SF1) held every quad map and every effective delta at
        ;; once and ran a 13 GB heap out (ayatori iteration 25); 250k per
        ;; batch bounds both, and the db value accumulates across batches
        quads (concat
               (for [[id [fname lname]] persons
                     x [(q id "person/firstName" fname) (q id "person/lastName" lname)]] x)
               (for [[id {:keys [gender birthday creationDate locationIP browserUsed]}] profiles
                     x [(q id "person/gender" gender) (q id "person/birthday" (str birthday))
                        (q id "person/creationDate" (str creationDate)) (q id "person/locationIP" locationIP)
                        (q id "person/browserUsed" browserUsed)]] x)
               (for [[p place] city] (q p "person/city" place))
               ;; undirected -> both directions; the friendship date reified per
               ;; direction (no edge properties): 3 datoms x 2 -- the data-model
               ;; cost IS3 pays, stated like `likes` below
               (for [[a b cd] knows
                     x (concat [(q a "knows" b) (q b "knows" a)]
                               (for [[x y] [[a b] [b a]]
                                     :let [kid (str "kn:" x ":" y)]
                                     z [(q kid "knows/person" x) (q kid "knows/friend" y) (q kid "knows/creationDate" (str cd))]] z))] x)
               (for [[post f] (:post->forum forums)] (q post "post/forum" f))
               (for [[f {:keys [title creationDate moderator]}] (:forums forums)
                     x (cond-> [(q f "forum/title" title) (q f "forum/creationDate" (str creationDate))]
                         moderator (conj (q f "forum/moderator" moderator)))] x)
               (for [[id [cd content]] messages
                     x [(q id "message/creationDate" (str cd)) (q id "message/content" (or content ""))]] x)
               (for [[m p] creator] (q m "hasCreator" p))
               (for [[c parent] reply-of] (q c "replyOf" parent))
               (for [[p m cd] likes                 ; reified: no edge properties
                     :let [lid (str "like:" p ":" m)]
                     x [(q lid "like/person" p) (q lid "like/message" m) (q lid "like/creationDate" (str cd))]] x))
        result (reduce (fn [{:keys [db n]} batch]
                         {:db (eng/transact db batch) :n (+ n (count batch))})
                       {:db (eng/empty-db) :n 0}
                       (partition-all 250000 quads))]
    {:db (:db result) :quads (:n result)}))

(def ^:private everything (constantly true))

(defn- top20-before
  "The date bound and the top-20 written INTO the query (ayatori iteration
  18): `[(< ?date max)]` is fused by the engine into a range on
  creationDate -- a keyed scan per bound message since datalog da95b877 --
  and `:order-by`/`:limit` do the ordering. The bound is the STRING form of
  max-date because creationDate is stored as a string here and the range
  compares like with like; every stored value is a 13-digit epoch-ms, so
  string order is date order. Rows come back `[x msg date-string]` and are
  returned as `[x msg date-long]`, the shape the Neo4j gate compares.
  The planner refuses to reorder a query with a predicate clause, so the
  clause order written here is the plan."
  [db where person max-date]
  (->> (eng/query db
                  {:find '[?x ?msg ?date]
                   :in '[?person]
                   :where (conj where [(list '< '?date (str max-date))])
                   :order-by '[[?date :desc] [?msg :asc]]
                   :limit 20}
                  everything [person])
       (mapv (fn [[x m d]] [x m (parse-long d)]))))

(defn- kb-ic02
  "IC02: the 20 most recent Messages by the start person's direct friends,
  created before max-date. Date bound and top-20 inside the query."
  [db person max-date]
  (top20-before db '[[?person "knows" ?x]
                     [?msg "hasCreator" ?x]
                     [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date]]
                person max-date))

(defn- kb-ic09
  "IC09: same as IC02 but over friends AND friends-of-friends, excluding the
  start person. TWO joins unioned, not one join plus a query per reachable
  person: a high-degree LDBC start person reaches ~1,300 people at two hops,
  and issuing 1,300 point queries would measure the harness's loop rather
  than the engine's join."
  [db person max-date]
  (let [one (top20-before db '[[?person "knows" ?x]
                               [?msg "hasCreator" ?x]
                               [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date]]
                          person max-date)
        ;; the two-hop half reaches the start person (a friend's friend is
        ;; you); the exclusion is INSIDE the query so the limit is taken
        ;; after it. Iteration 18 excluded afterwards, which cut rows the
        ;; answer needed whenever the start person's own messages sat in the
        ;; two-hop top 20 -- never on the subset (every official IC09 was
        ;; empty there), caught by the startup gate on full SF1 (iteration
        ;; 25, one parameter). The union's top 20 is a subset of the union of
        ;; the two top-20s, so limiting each is exact once both are filtered.
        two (top20-before db '[[?person "knows" ?f1]
                               [?f1 "knows" ?x]
                               [(not= ?x ?person)]
                               [?msg "hasCreator" ?x]
                               [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date]]
                          person max-date)]
    (->> (into one two)
         distinct
         (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) second))
         (take 20) vec)))

(defn- kb-ic07
  "IC07: the 20 most recent likes on the start person's Messages, one row per
  liker (their latest like)."
  [db person]
  (->> (eng/query db
                  '{:find [?liker ?msg ?date]
                    :in [?person]
                    :where [[?msg "hasCreator" ?person]
                            [?like "like/message" ?msg]
                            [?like "like/person" ?liker]
                            [?like "like/creationDate" ?date]]}
                  everything [person])
       (map (fn [[l m d]] [l m (parse-long d)]))
       (group-by first)
       (map (fn [[_ ls]] (first (sort-by (comp - #(nth % 2)) ls))))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) first))
       (take 20) vec))

(defn- kb-ic08
  "IC08: the 20 most recent Comments that are direct replies to the start
  person's Messages."
  [db person]
  (->> (eng/query db
                  '{:find [?author ?reply ?date]
                    :in [?person]
                    :where [[?msg "hasCreator" ?person]
                            [?reply "replyOf" ?msg]
                            [?reply "hasCreator" ?author]
                            [?reply "message/creationDate" ?date]]}
                  everything [person])
       (map (fn [[a r d]] [a r (parse-long d)]))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) second))
       (take 20) vec))

;; ── kotobase side, index path ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;; The same four reads written against the datom plane's index access paths
;; (`entity` = EAVT, `datoms :avet [a v]` = AVET point lookup) instead of the
;; Datalog join. ADR-2608039970: a surface whose algebra binds directly to L1
;; index access may do so; `bridge/q` is an option, not the default. These
;; exist because the co-scientist loop (ayatori iteration 16, 2026-09-11)
;; found IC09 = 71% and IC08 = 25% of the SUT's CPU at its throughput
;; ceiling, most of it in `datalog.core/hash-join-rows` building a broad
;; index per query. Every one of these returns the SAME rows as its Datalog
;; twin above -- `index-path-agrees?` checks that on real parameters and the
;; net server refuses to serve the index path if it does not.

(defn- by-avet
  "Subjects with `[s p o]` -- the AVET point lookup. Straight off the index
  (arrangement.core/by-predicate-value, a set of subjects): the public
  `eng/datoms` route built one `{:e :a :v_edn :added}` map per subject and
  wire-encoded the value, which was 20-30% of the IC09 twin on 80k messages
  (ayatori iteration 26)."
  [db p o]
  (qs/by-predicate-value db p o))

(defn- top-k-by
  "The `k` smallest rows under `cmp`, kept in a bounded sorted set as rows
  stream by -- so a twin that sees 80k candidate rows sorts 20, not 80k."
  [k cmp rows]
  (vec (reduce (fn [acc r]
                 (let [acc (conj acc r)]
                   (if (> (count acc) k) (disj acc (first (rseq acc))) acc)))
               (sorted-set-by cmp)
               rows)))

(def ^:private newest-first-then-msg
  "creationDate desc, message id asc -- the LDBC tie rule, as a comparator on
  `[x msg date]` rows; distinct rows never compare equal (msg is a key)."
  (fn [[xa ma da] [xb mb db2]]
    (let [c (compare db2 da)] (if (zero? c) (let [c2 (compare ma mb)] (if (zero? c2) (compare xa xb) c2)) c))))

(defn- attr1 [db s a] (first (get (eng/entity db s) a)))

(defn- msg-rows
  "[creator msg date] for every Message by `creator` with date < max-date."
  [db creator max-date]
  (keep (fn [m] (let [d (parse-long (attr1 db m "message/creationDate"))]
                  (when (< d max-date) [creator m d])))
        (by-avet db "hasCreator" creator)))

(defn- kb-ic02-index [db person max-date]
  (->> (get (eng/entity db person) "knows")
       (mapcat #(msg-rows db % max-date))
       (top-k-by 20 newest-first-then-msg)))

(defn- kb-ic09-index [db person max-date]
  (let [friends (get (eng/entity db person) "knows" #{})
        fof (into friends (mapcat #(get (eng/entity db %) "knows")) friends)]
    (->> (disj fof person)
         (mapcat #(msg-rows db % max-date))
         (top-k-by 20 newest-first-then-msg))))

(defn- kb-ic07-index [db person]
  (->> (by-avet db "hasCreator" person)
       (mapcat (fn [m] (map (fn [like] [(attr1 db like "like/person") m
                                         (parse-long (attr1 db like "like/creationDate"))])
                            (by-avet db "like/message" m))))
       (group-by first)
       (map (fn [[_ ls]] (first (sort-by (comp - #(nth % 2)) ls))))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) first))
       (take 20) vec))

(defn- kb-ic08-index [db person]
  (->> (by-avet db "hasCreator" person)
       (mapcat (fn [m] (map (fn [r] [(attr1 db r "hasCreator") r
                                      (parse-long (attr1 db r "message/creationDate"))])
                            (by-avet db "replyOf" m))))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) second))
       (take 20) vec))

;; ── short reads IS2 / IS4 / IS5 / IS7 (ayatori iteration 22) ─────────────
;; LDBC SNB Interactive v1 short reads on the attributes this subset loads
;; (names, message content and creationDate, hasCreator, replyOf, knows).
;; IS1 (profile), IS3 (friends with friendship date) and IS6 (forum) need
;; attributes the subset does not load and are not served. Each read is
;; written twice, Datalog and index path, and gated like the complex reads.

(defn- root-post
  "Follow replyOf up to the message with no replyOf (the original Post)."
  [db m]
  (loop [m m n 0]
    (let [parent (attr1 db m "replyOf")]
      (if (or (nil? parent) (> n 64)) m (recur parent (inc n))))))

(defn- person-name [db p]
  (let [e (eng/entity db p)]
    [(first (get e "person/firstName")) (first (get e "person/lastName"))]))

(defn- kb-is2
  "IS2: the 10 most recent Messages by the person -- [msg content date
  root-post root-author root-first root-last], newest first, id desc on ties."
  [db person]
  (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?m ?c ?d] :in '[?p]
                      :where '[[?m "hasCreator" ?p] [?m "message/creationDate" ?d] [?m "message/content" ?c]]
                      :order-by '[[?d :desc] [?m :desc]] :limit 10}
                  everything [person])
       (mapv (fn [[m c d]]
               (let [root (root-post db m) author (attr1 db root "hasCreator")
                     [f l] (person-name db author)]
                 [m c (parse-long d) root author f l])))))

(defn- kb-is2-index [db person]
  (->> (by-avet db "hasCreator" person)
       (map (fn [m] [m (attr1 db m "message/content") (parse-long (attr1 db m "message/creationDate"))]))
       (sort-by (fn [[m _ d]] [(- d) m]) (fn [[da ma] [db2 mb]] (let [c (compare da db2)] (if (zero? c) (compare mb ma) c))))
       (take 10)
       (mapv (fn [[m c d]]
               (let [root (root-post db m) author (attr1 db root "hasCreator")
                     [f l] (person-name db author)]
                 [m c d root author f l])))))

(defn- kb-is4
  "IS4: a message's creationDate and content -- [date content] or []."
  [db message]
  (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?d ?c] :in '[?m]
                      :where '[[?m "message/creationDate" ?d] [?m "message/content" ?c]]}
                  everything [message])
       (mapv (fn [[d c]] [(parse-long d) c]))))

(defn- kb-is4-index [db message]
  (let [e (eng/entity db message)]
    (if-let [d (first (get e "message/creationDate"))]
      [[(parse-long d) (first (get e "message/content"))]]
      [])))

(defn- kb-is5
  "IS5: a message's creator -- [person first last] or []."
  [db message]
  (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?p ?f ?l] :in '[?m]
                      :where '[[?m "hasCreator" ?p] [?p "person/firstName" ?f] [?p "person/lastName" ?l]]}
                  everything [message])
       vec))

(defn- kb-is5-index [db message]
  (if-let [p (attr1 db message "hasCreator")]
    (let [[f l] (person-name db p)] [[p f l]])
    []))

(defn- kb-is7
  "IS7: the replies to a message -- [reply content date author first last
  knows?], newest first, author asc on ties; knows? = the reply's author and
  the message's author know each other."
  [db message]
  (let [author (attr1 db message "hasCreator")
        friends (get (eng/entity db author) "knows" #{})]
    (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?r ?c ?d ?a ?f ?l] :in '[?m]
                        :where '[[?r "replyOf" ?m] [?r "hasCreator" ?a] [?r "message/creationDate" ?d]
                                 [?r "message/content" ?c] [?a "person/firstName" ?f] [?a "person/lastName" ?l]]}
                    everything [message])
         (map (fn [[r c d a f l]] [r c (parse-long d) a f l (contains? friends a)]))
         (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) #(nth % 3)))
         vec)))

(defn- kb-is7-index [db message]
  (let [author (attr1 db message "hasCreator")
        friends (get (eng/entity db author) "knows" #{})]
    (->> (by-avet db "replyOf" message)
         (map (fn [r] (let [e (eng/entity db r) a (first (get e "hasCreator")) [f l] (person-name db a)]
                        [r (first (get e "message/content")) (parse-long (first (get e "message/creationDate"))) a f l (contains? friends a)])))
         (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 2)) #(nth % 3)))
         vec)))

(defn- kb-is1
  "IS1: a person's profile -- [first last birthday locationIP browserUsed
  city gender creationDate] or []."
  [db person]
  (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?f ?l ?b ?ip ?br ?c ?g ?cd] :in '[?p]
                      :where '[[?p "person/firstName" ?f] [?p "person/lastName" ?l] [?p "person/birthday" ?b]
                               [?p "person/locationIP" ?ip] [?p "person/browserUsed" ?br] [?p "person/city" ?c]
                               [?p "person/gender" ?g] [?p "person/creationDate" ?cd]]}
                  everything [person])
       (mapv (fn [[f l b ip br c g cd]] [f l (parse-long b) ip br c g (parse-long cd)]))))

(defn- kb-is1-index [db person]
  (let [e (eng/entity db person) one (fn [a] (first (get e a)))]
    (if (one "person/firstName")
      [[(one "person/firstName") (one "person/lastName") (parse-long (one "person/birthday")) (one "person/locationIP")
        (one "person/browserUsed") (one "person/city") (one "person/gender") (parse-long (one "person/creationDate"))]]
      [])))

(defn- kb-is3
  "IS3: a person's friends -- [friend first last friendshipDate], newest
  friendship first, friend id asc on ties."
  [db person]
  (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?f ?fn ?ln ?d] :in '[?p]
                      :where '[[?k "knows/person" ?p] [?k "knows/friend" ?f] [?k "knows/creationDate" ?d]
                               [?f "person/firstName" ?fn] [?f "person/lastName" ?ln]]}
                  everything [person])
       (map (fn [[f fn ln d]] [f fn ln (parse-long d)]))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 3)) first))
       vec))

(defn- kb-is3-index [db person]
  (->> (by-avet db "knows/person" person)
       (map (fn [k] (let [f (attr1 db k "knows/friend") [fn ln] (person-name db f)]
                      [f fn ln (parse-long (attr1 db k "knows/creationDate"))])))
       (sort-by (juxt (comp - #(nth % 3)) first))
       vec))

(defn- kb-is6
  "IS6: the forum of a message (of its root post) -- [forum title
  moderator first last] or []."
  [db message]
  (let [root (root-post db message)]
    (->> (eng/query db {:find '[?f ?t ?m ?fn ?ln] :in '[?post]
                        :where '[[?post "post/forum" ?f] [?f "forum/title" ?t] [?f "forum/moderator" ?m]
                                 [?m "person/firstName" ?fn] [?m "person/lastName" ?ln]]}
                    everything [root])
         vec)))

(defn- kb-is6-index [db message]
  (let [root (root-post db message) f (attr1 db root "post/forum")]
    (if-let [m (and f (attr1 db f "forum/moderator"))]
      (let [[fn ln] (person-name db m)] [[f (attr1 db f "forum/title") m fn ln]])
      [])))

(defn index-path-agrees?
  "For every `{:op :person :max-date}` in `params`, the index path returns the
  rows the Datalog path returns (ordered rows for IC02/IC08; IC07 compares
  liker sets and IC09 message sets, the same tie rule the Neo4j gate uses).
  Returns `{:checked n :disagreements [...]}` -- a count, so one disagreement
  and a broken run are different answers."
  [db max-date params]
  (let [one (fn [{:keys [op person] :as p}]
              (let [md (or (:max-date p) max-date)]
                (case op
                  :ic02 (= (kb-ic02 db person md) (kb-ic02-index db person md))
                  :ic07 (= (set (map first (kb-ic07 db person))) (set (map first (kb-ic07-index db person))))
                  :ic08 (= (kb-ic08 db person) (kb-ic08-index db person))
                  :ic09 (= (set (map second (kb-ic09 db person md))) (set (map second (kb-ic09-index db person md))))
                  :is2 (= (kb-is2 db person) (kb-is2-index db person))
                  :is4 (= (kb-is4 db (:message p)) (kb-is4-index db (:message p)))
                  :is5 (= (kb-is5 db (:message p)) (kb-is5-index db (:message p)))
                  :is7 (= (kb-is7 db (:message p)) (kb-is7-index db (:message p)))
                  :is1 (= (kb-is1 db person) (kb-is1-index db person))
                  :is3 (= (kb-is3 db person) (kb-is3-index db person))
                  :is6 (= (kb-is6 db (:message p)) (kb-is6-index db (:message p))))))]
    {:checked (count params)
     :disagreements (vec (remove one params))}))

;; ── neo4j side ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(def ^:private cy-ic02
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})-[:KNOWS]-(f:Person)<-[:HAS_CREATOR]-(m:Message)
   WHERE m.creationDate < $maxDate
   RETURN f.pid AS friend, m.pid AS msg, m.creationDate AS date
   ORDER BY date DESC, msg ASC LIMIT 20")

(def ^:private cy-ic09
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})-[:KNOWS*1..2]-(f:Person)<-[:HAS_CREATOR]-(m:Message)
   WHERE m.creationDate < $maxDate AND f <> p
   RETURN DISTINCT f.pid AS friend, m.pid AS msg, m.creationDate AS date
   ORDER BY date DESC, msg ASC LIMIT 20")

(def ^:private cy-ic07
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})<-[:HAS_CREATOR]-(m:Message)<-[l:LIKES]-(liker:Person)
   WITH liker, m, l.creationDate AS date
   ORDER BY date DESC
   WITH liker, collect({m:m.pid, d:date})[0] AS top
   RETURN liker.pid AS liker, top.m AS msg, top.d AS date
   ORDER BY date DESC, liker ASC LIMIT 20")

(def ^:private cy-ic08
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})<-[:HAS_CREATOR]-(m:Message)<-[:REPLY_OF]-(r:Message)-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(a:Person)
   RETURN a.pid AS author, r.pid AS reply, r.creationDate AS date
   ORDER BY date DESC, reply ASC LIMIT 20")

(def ^:private cy-is2
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})<-[:HAS_CREATOR]-(m:Message)
   WITH m ORDER BY m.creationDate DESC, m.pid DESC LIMIT 10
   MATCH (m)-[:REPLY_OF*0..]->(root:Message) WHERE NOT (root)-[:REPLY_OF]->()
   MATCH (root)-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(a:Person)
   RETURN m.pid AS msg, m.content AS content, m.creationDate AS date,
          root.pid AS root, a.pid AS author, a.firstName AS f, a.lastName AS l
   ORDER BY date DESC, msg DESC")

(def ^:private cy-is4
  "MATCH (m:Message {pid:$mid}) RETURN m.creationDate AS date, m.content AS content")

(def ^:private cy-is5
  "MATCH (m:Message {pid:$mid})-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(p:Person)
   RETURN p.pid AS person, p.firstName AS f, p.lastName AS l")

(def ^:private cy-is7
  "MATCH (m:Message {pid:$mid})<-[:REPLY_OF]-(r:Message)-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(a:Person)
   MATCH (m)-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(ma:Person)
   RETURN r.pid AS reply, r.content AS content, r.creationDate AS date,
          a.pid AS author, a.firstName AS f, a.lastName AS l,
          EXISTS { (a)-[:KNOWS]-(ma) } AS knows
   ORDER BY date DESC, author ASC")

(def ^:private cy-is1
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})
   RETURN p.firstName AS f, p.lastName AS l, p.birthday AS b, p.locationIP AS ip,
          p.browserUsed AS br, p.city AS c, p.gender AS g, p.creationDate AS cd")

(def ^:private cy-is3
  "MATCH (p:Person {pid:$pid})-[k:KNOWS]-(f:Person)
   RETURN f.pid AS friend, f.firstName AS fn, f.lastName AS ln, k.creationDate AS d
   ORDER BY d DESC, friend ASC")

(def ^:private cy-is6
  "MATCH (m:Message {pid:$mid})-[:REPLY_OF*0..]->(root:Message) WHERE NOT (root)-[:REPLY_OF]->()
   MATCH (fo:Forum)-[:CONTAINER_OF]->(root) MATCH (fo)-[:HAS_MODERATOR]->(mod:Person)
   RETURN fo.pid AS forum, fo.title AS title, mod.pid AS mod, mod.firstName AS fn, mod.lastName AS ln")

(defn- batched!
  "Run one parameterised write per chunk, each in its OWN transaction --
  a single transaction holding half a million relationship creates is a
  memory problem, not a benchmark."
  [gdb q rows]
  (doseq [chunk (partition-all 20000 rows)]
    (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
      (.execute tx q {"rows" (vec chunk)})
      (.commit tx))))

(defn- neo4j-load!
  "Nodes, then constraints (own transaction -- Neo4j forbids schema and data
  changes in the same tx), then await online, then relationships.

  Two things here are load-bearing and were both wrong in the first draft:

  UNIQUENESS CONSTRAINTS, not plain indexes. A unique constraint gives the
  planner a UniqueIndexSeek it will always prefer; a plain index on a label
  whose statistics are still empty right after creation can lose to a
  NodeByLabelScan.

  SEPARATE MATCH CLAUSES, not `MATCH (a:X {..}), (b:Y {..})`. The comma form
  is planned as a cartesian product of two node scans unless the planner
  pushes both property predicates into seeks; when it does not, a 20,000-row
  batch turns into 20,000 x 2 x |label| node touches. Measured symptom: the
  relationship phase ran for tens of minutes at ~20% of one core with the
  store file not growing at all. Two separate MATCH clauses give the planner
  no cartesian option to choose."
  [gdb {:keys [persons knows messages creator reply-of likes profiles city forums]}]
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r CREATE (:Person {pid:r.pid, firstName:r.f, lastName:r.l, "
                     "gender:r.g, birthday:r.b, creationDate:r.cd, locationIP:r.ip, browserUsed:r.br, city:r.city})")
            (mapv (fn [[id [f l]]] (let [pr (get profiles id)]
                                     {"pid" id "f" f "l" l "g" (:gender pr) "b" (:birthday pr) "cd" (:creationDate pr)
                                      "ip" (:locationIP pr) "br" (:browserUsed pr) "city" (get city id)}))
                  persons))
  (batched! gdb "UNWIND $rows AS r CREATE (:Forum {pid:r.pid, title:r.t, creationDate:r.cd})"
            (mapv (fn [[f {:keys [title creationDate]}]] {"pid" f "t" title "cd" creationDate}) (:forums forums)))
  (batched! gdb "UNWIND $rows AS r CREATE (:Message {pid:r.pid, creationDate:r.d, content:r.c})"
            (mapv (fn [[id [d c]]] {"pid" id "d" d "c" (or c "")}) messages))
  (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
    (-> (.schema tx) (.constraintFor (Label/label "Person")) (.assertPropertyIsUnique "pid") (.create))
    (-> (.schema tx) (.constraintFor (Label/label "Message")) (.assertPropertyIsUnique "pid") (.create))
    (.commit tx))
  (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
    (-> (.schema tx) (.awaitIndexesOnline 600 TimeUnit/SECONDS)))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (a:Person {pid:r.a}) MATCH (b:Person {pid:r.b}) "
                     "CREATE (a)-[:KNOWS {creationDate:r.d}]->(b)")
            (mapv (fn [[a b d]] {"a" a "b" b "d" d}) knows))
  (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
    (-> (.schema tx) (.constraintFor (Label/label "Forum")) (.assertPropertyIsUnique "pid") (.create))
    (.commit tx))
  (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
    (-> (.schema tx) (.awaitIndexesOnline 600 TimeUnit/SECONDS)))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (f:Forum {pid:r.f}) MATCH (m:Message {pid:r.m}) "
                     "CREATE (f)-[:CONTAINER_OF]->(m)")
            (mapv (fn [[post f]] {"f" f "m" post}) (:post->forum forums)))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (f:Forum {pid:r.f}) MATCH (p:Person {pid:r.p}) "
                     "CREATE (f)-[:HAS_MODERATOR]->(p)")
            (vec (for [[f {:keys [moderator]}] (:forums forums) :when moderator] {"f" f "p" moderator})))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (m:Message {pid:r.m}) MATCH (p:Person {pid:r.p}) "
                     "CREATE (m)-[:HAS_CREATOR]->(p)")
            (mapv (fn [[m p]] {"m" m "p" p}) creator))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (c:Message {pid:r.c}) MATCH (p:Message {pid:r.p}) "
                     "CREATE (c)-[:REPLY_OF]->(p)")
            (mapv (fn [[c p]] {"c" c "p" p}) reply-of))
  (batched! gdb (str "UNWIND $rows AS r "
                     "MATCH (p:Person {pid:r.p}) MATCH (m:Message {pid:r.m}) "
                     "CREATE (p)-[:LIKES {creationDate:r.d}]->(m)")
            (mapv (fn [[p m d]] {"p" p "m" m "d" d}) likes)))

(defn- neo4j-rows [tx cypher params]
  (with-open [res (.execute tx cypher params)]
    (mapv (fn [^java.util.Map row] (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(keyword k) v])) row))
          (iterator-seq res))))

;; ── measurement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- elapsed-ms [f]
  (let [start (System/nanoTime) result (f)]
    {:ms (/ (- (System/nanoTime) start) 1e6) :result result}))

(defn- percentile [xs q]
  (let [s (vec (sort xs))]
    (nth s (min (dec (count s)) (long (Math/floor (* q (count s))))))))

(defn- stats [xs]
  {:p50-ms (percentile xs 0.50) :p95-ms (percentile xs 0.95)
   :p99-ms (percentile xs 0.99)
   :mean-ms (/ (reduce + xs) (double (count xs)))
   :samples (count xs)})

(defn- pick-persons
  "Deterministic start-person set: the persons with the most `knows` edges,
  which is where LDBC's own parameter curation aims (a start person with no
  friends measures nothing). Sorted by id for reproducibility."
  [{:keys [knows]} n]
  (let [deg (reduce (fn [m [a b]] (-> m (update a (fnil inc 0)) (update b (fnil inc 0)))) {} knows)]
    (->> deg (sort-by (juxt (comp - val) key)) (take n) (mapv key) sort vec)))

(defn- read-param-lines [params-dir file]
  (->> (str/split-lines (slurp (str params-dir "/" file))) rest (remove empty?) vec))

(defn reference-agreement
  "Every served read against Neo4j on the OFFICIAL parameters (and this
  subset's short-read message ids), not only on the first start person:
  `{op {:checked n :disagreements [...]}}`. IC02/IC08/IS2/IS4/IS5/IS7 compare
  ordered rows; IC07 liker sets and IC09 message sets (creationDate ties at
  the limit boundary, as the first-person gate already does). A count per
  op, so one disagreement and a broken run are different answers."
  [db tx max-date params-dir]
  (let [split (fn [line] (str/split line #"\|"))
        norm-kb (fn [rows] (mapv (fn [r] [(str (first r)) (str (second r)) (nth r 2)]) rows))
        norm-neo (fn [rows ka kb] (mapv (fn [r] [(str (get r ka)) (str (get r kb)) (:date r)]) rows))
        ic (fn [n f] (let [lines (read-param-lines params-dir (str "interactive_" n "_param.txt"))
                           bad (remove (fn [line] (let [[p md] (split line)] (f p (if md (parse-long md) max-date)))) lines)]
                       {:checked (count lines) :disagreements (vec bad)}))
        msgs (read-param-lines params-dir "short_message_param.txt")
        is (fn [f] (let [bad (remove f msgs)] {:checked (count msgs) :disagreements (vec bad)}))
        is2-persons (read-param-lines params-dir "interactive_7_param.txt")]
    {:ic02 (ic 2 (fn [p md] (= (norm-kb (kb-ic02 db p md)) (norm-neo (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic02 {"pid" p "maxDate" md}) :friend :msg))))
     :ic07 (ic 7 (fn [p _] (= (set (map first (kb-ic07 db p))) (set (map (comp str :liker) (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic07 {"pid" p}))))))
     :ic08 (ic 8 (fn [p _] (= (norm-kb (kb-ic08 db p)) (norm-neo (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic08 {"pid" p}) :author :reply))))
     :ic09 (ic 9 (fn [p md] (= (set (map second (kb-ic09 db p md))) (set (map (comp str :msg) (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic09 {"pid" p "maxDate" md}))))))
     :is2 (let [bad (remove (fn [p] (= (kb-is2 db p)
                                       (mapv (fn [r] [(str (:msg r)) (:content r) (:date r) (str (:root r)) (str (:author r)) (:f r) (:l r)])
                                             (neo4j-rows tx cy-is2 {"pid" p}))))
                            is2-persons)]
            {:checked (count is2-persons) :disagreements (vec bad)})
     :is4 (is (fn [m] (= (kb-is4 db m) (mapv (fn [r] [(:date r) (:content r)]) (neo4j-rows tx cy-is4 {"mid" m})))))
     :is5 (is (fn [m] (= (kb-is5 db m) (mapv (fn [r] [(str (:person r)) (:f r) (:l r)]) (neo4j-rows tx cy-is5 {"mid" m})))))
     :is1 (let [bad (remove (fn [p] (= (kb-is1 db p)
                                       (mapv (fn [r] [(:f r) (:l r) (:b r) (:ip r) (:br r) (str (:c r)) (:g r) (:cd r)])
                                             (neo4j-rows tx cy-is1 {"pid" p}))))
                            is2-persons)]
            {:checked (count is2-persons) :disagreements (vec bad)})
     :is3 (let [bad (remove (fn [p] (= (kb-is3 db p)
                                       (mapv (fn [r] [(str (:friend r)) (:fn r) (:ln r) (:d r)])
                                             (neo4j-rows tx cy-is3 {"pid" p}))))
                            is2-persons)]
            {:checked (count is2-persons) :disagreements (vec bad)})
     :is6 (is (fn [m] (= (kb-is6 db m)
                         (mapv (fn [r] [(str (:forum r)) (:title r) (str (:mod r)) (:fn r) (:ln r)])
                               (neo4j-rows tx cy-is6 {"mid" m})))))
     :is7 (is (fn [m] (= (kb-is7 db m)
                         (mapv (fn [r] [(str (:reply r)) (:content r) (:date r) (str (:author r)) (:f r) (:l r) (boolean (:knows r))])
                               (neo4j-rows tx cy-is7 {"mid" m})))))}))

(defn -main [& args]
  (let [dir (or (first args)
                (throw (ex-info "usage: clojure -M:ldbc-snb-bench <ldbc-dynamic-dir> [posts] [samples]" {})))
        n-posts (parse-long (or (second args) "35000"))
        samples (parse-long (or (nth args 2 nil) "30"))
        params-dir (nth args 3 nil)
        load-t (elapsed-ms #(load-subset dir n-posts))
        data (:result load-t)
        max-date (+ 1 (reduce max 0 (keep first (vals (:messages data)))))
        starts (pick-persons data samples)
        elements {:persons (count (:persons data))
                  :knows-edges (count (:knows data))
                  :messages (count (:messages data))
                  :posts (count (:post-ids data))
                  :comments (count (:comment-ids data))
                  :has-creator (count (:creator data))
                  :reply-of (count (:reply-of data))
                  :likes (count (:likes data))}
        elements (assoc elements :total (reduce + (vals (dissoc elements :posts :comments))))
        kb-build (elapsed-ms #(build-kotobase data))
        {:keys [db quads]} (:result kb-build)
        tmp (.toFile (Files/createTempDirectory "ldbc-neo4j" (make-array FileAttribute 0)))
        dbms (.build (DatabaseManagementServiceBuilder. (.toPath tmp)))
        gdb (.database dbms "neo4j")
        neo-load (elapsed-ms #(neo4j-load! gdb data))
        ;; ---- correctness: the two engines must agree before any timing counts
        agreement
        (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
          (let [p (first starts)
                norm-kb (fn [rows] (mapv (fn [r] [(str (first r)) (str (second r)) (nth r 2)]) rows))
                norm-neo (fn [rows ka kb] (mapv (fn [r] [(str (get r ka)) (str (get r kb)) (:date r)]) rows))]
            {:ic02 (= (norm-kb (kb-ic02 db p max-date))
                      (norm-neo (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic02 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date}) :friend :msg))
             :ic07 (= (set (map first (kb-ic07 db p)))
                      (set (map (comp str :liker) (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic07 {"pid" p}))))
             :ic08 (= (norm-kb (kb-ic08 db p))
                      (norm-neo (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic08 {"pid" p}) :author :reply))
             :ic09 (= (set (map second (kb-ic09 db p max-date)))
                      (set (map (comp str :msg) (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic09 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date}))))}))
        ;; ---- the same, over every official parameter (and the subset's
        ;; short-read messages) when a params dir is given -- the reference
        ;; the network rows cite (ayatori iteration 23)
        official-agreement (when params-dir
                             (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
                               (reference-agreement db tx max-date params-dir)))
        ;; ---- warm both engines
        _ (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
            (doseq [p (take 5 starts)]
              (kb-ic02 db p max-date) (kb-ic07 db p) (kb-ic08 db p) (kb-ic09 db p max-date)
              (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic02 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date})
              (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic07 {"pid" p})
              (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic08 {"pid" p})
              (neo4j-rows tx cy-ic09 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date})))
        ;; INTERLEAVED, one start person at a time, both engines before moving
        ;; on. This used to run all 15 kotobase samples and THEN all 15 Neo4j
        ;; samples, which meant the two halves saw different minutes of a shared
        ;; machine -- and the kb/neo ratio was presented in every receipt as the
        ;; load-invariant column precisely because both engines were supposed to
        ;; be measured under the same conditions.
        ;;
        ;; They were not. Measured (bench/results/
        ;; 2026-08-02-limit-pushdown-third-attempt.edn): across two runs of
        ;; BYTE-IDENTICAL kotobase code, IC07's kotobase side went 6.4 -> 16.1 ms
        ;; while its Neo4j side went 1.4 -> 1.2 ms, moving the ratio 4.7x ->
        ;; 13.9x with nothing changed. That is the defect this fixes, and it
        ;; invalidates 20-30% movements in every ratio measured before it.
        both (with-open [tx (.beginTx gdb)]
               (reduce
                (fn [acc p]
                  (-> acc
                      (update-in [:kb :ic02] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(kb-ic02 db p max-date))))
                      (update-in [:neo :ic02] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(neo4j-rows tx cy-ic02 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date}))))
                      (update-in [:kb :ic07] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(kb-ic07 db p))))
                      (update-in [:neo :ic07] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(neo4j-rows tx cy-ic07 {"pid" p}))))
                      (update-in [:kb :ic08] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(kb-ic08 db p))))
                      (update-in [:neo :ic08] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(neo4j-rows tx cy-ic08 {"pid" p}))))
                      (update-in [:kb :ic09] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(kb-ic09 db p max-date))))
                      (update-in [:neo :ic09] conj (:ms (elapsed-ms #(neo4j-rows tx cy-ic09 {"pid" p "maxDate" max-date}))))))
                {:kb {:ic02 [] :ic07 [] :ic08 [] :ic09 []}
                 :neo {:ic02 [] :ic07 [] :ic08 [] :ic09 []}}
                starts))
        kb-times (:kb both)
        neo-times (:neo both)
        ;; The ratio taken PER SAMPLE and then summarised, not as a quotient of
        ;; two independently-summarised numbers. A per-sample ratio cancels
        ;; whatever the machine was doing during that pair; a quotient of
        ;; medians does not.
        paired-ratios (into {}
                            (map (fn [k]
                                   [k (stats (mapv / (get kb-times k) (get neo-times k)))]))
                            [:ic02 :ic07 :ic08 :ic09])]
    (.shutdown dbms)
    (prn
     {:schema 1
      :receipt/type :ldbc-snb-interactive
      :implementation "kotobase-peer.ldbc-snb-bench"
      :dataset {:name "LDBC SNB Interactive v1, SF1, Datagen v1.0.0, CsvComposite/LongDateFormatter"
                :source "https://repository.surfsara.nl/datasets/cwi/ldbc-snb-interactive-v1-datagen-v100"
                :subset-rule (str "all persons + all knows; " n-posts " most recent Posts + full transitive reply trees; "
                                  "hasCreator for included Messages; likes onto included Messages")
                :elements elements
                :load-ms (:ms load-t)}
      :kotobase {:quads quads
                 :build-ms (:ms kb-build)
                 :note "hot db via eng/transact -- same db value the persisted path hydrates to; no AES-GCM/content-addressing in this number"
                 :ic02 (stats (:ic02 kb-times)) :ic07 (stats (:ic07 kb-times))
                 :ic08 (stats (:ic08 kb-times)) :ic09 (stats (:ic09 kb-times))}
      :neo4j-embedded {:load-ms (:ms neo-load)
                       :version "org.neo4j/neo4j 2025.03.0 embedded (community), temp on-disk store, pid UNIQUE constraints awaited online"
                       :ic02 (stats (:ic02 neo-times)) :ic07 (stats (:ic07 neo-times))
                       :ic08 (stats (:ic08 neo-times)) :ic09 (stats (:ic09 neo-times))}
      :answers-agree agreement
      :answers-agree-official (when official-agreement
                                (into {} (map (fn [[op v]] [op (assoc v :disagreements (count (:disagreements v))
                                                                          :first-disagreement (first (:disagreements v)))]))
                                      official-agreement))
      :paired-ratio
      (assoc paired-ratios
             :note "kotobase-ms / neo4j-ms computed PER SAMPLE with the two engines run back to back, then summarised. This is the column to compare across runs; a quotient of two separately-summarised medians is not, which is what every receipt before 2026-08-02 used.")
      :start-persons {:n (count starts) :rule "highest knows-degree, sorted by id"}
      :caveats
      ["Subset of SF1, not full SF1 (~10.5M elements) -- the subset rule is stated above and is deterministic."
       "kotobase has no ORDER BY/LIMIT in its query language; the top-20 step runs in harness Clojure over the join's full result, while Neo4j does it inside Cypher. Both are timed to the same final answer and the answers are asserted equal."
       "kotobase stores knows in both directions (no undirected edge) and reifies likes (no edge properties); Neo4j uses a native undirected match and a relationship property."
       "kotobase's number is a hot in-memory db with no encryption and no persistence; the persisted path additionally pays hydration, which is measured in ADR-2607310900 and the dag-shape receipt, not here."
       "Neo4j embedded has no pure in-memory mode; its temp on-disk store is read through the page cache after warmup."
       "Single machine, single JVM, no concurrency. NamiDB's published numbers are on different hardware -- only the ratio against the shared Neo4j yardstick is comparable, and only loosely."
       "IC07/IC09 agreement compares result SETS (likers / message ids) rather than ordered rows: both queries can have creationDate ties at the LIMIT-20 boundary, where the two engines are free to pick different rows. IC02/IC08 are compared as ordered rows. A reported disagreement is reported, never relaxed after the fact."]})))
